/** @page mysqlerrorinfoguide Error Information

    @tableofcontents

    Every exception raised for an error reported by MySQL or by the MySQL client library carries an
    argument hash with the following keys, so that errors can be classified from machine-readable
    codes instead of from the server's localized error text:

    |!Key|!Type|!Description
    |\c alterr|<tt>Type::String</tt>|the five-character \c SQLSTATE for the error (for example, \c "40001"); an empty string if the client library reports no \c SQLSTATE
    |\c alterr_code|<tt>Type::Int</tt>|the MySQL-specific error number (for example, \c 1213 for \c ER_LOCK_DEADLOCK)
    |\c alterr_diag|<tt>Type::String</tt>|the diagnostic text for the error as reported by MySQL

    All three keys are always present, so that they can be read without testing for their presence
    first.

    Both the \c SQLSTATE and the error number are needed to classify an error; the \c SQLSTATE alone
    is not sufficient, because MySQL reports many unrelated errors with the generic value
    \c "HY000".  A lock wait timeout (\c ER_LOCK_WAIT_TIMEOUT, \c 1205) is one such error, while a
    deadlock (\c ER_LOCK_DEADLOCK, \c 1213) is reported with \c SQLSTATE \c "40001".

    @note Errors raised for a prepared statement report the error from the statement rather than
    from the connection; a client-side statement error is never reported on the connection at all.

    A deadlock aborts and rolls back the entire transaction, so the canonical handling is to replay
    the complete transaction:

    @code{.py}
while (True) {
    try {
        on_error ds.rollback();
        on_success ds.commit();
        # the complete transaction body must be idempotent
        doTransaction(ds);
        break;
    } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
        if (ex.arg.alterr_code != 1213) {
            rethrow;
        }
    }
}
    @endcode
*/
